The linux-dmabuf format_table event passes ownership of the fd to the client. xdph mmaps it and
never closes it, so every re-sent feedback leaks one descriptor on an unlinked /dev/shm file. Once
the first feedback has completed, dma.done short-circuits the handler and leaks the fd without even
mapping it. At the 1024-fd default this kills every later ScreenCast session with EMFILE
("Failed to create event object (Too many open files)") after roughly a day of uptime.

The mapping is MAP_PRIVATE and stays valid after the descriptor is closed.

--- a/src/core/PortalManager.cpp
+++ b/src/core/PortalManager.cpp
@@ -151,13 +151,17 @@
         m_sWaylandConnection.linuxDmabufFeedback->setFormatTable([this](CCZwpLinuxDmabufFeedbackV1* r, int fd, uint32_t size) {
             Debug::log(TRACE, "[core] dmabufFeedbackFormatTable");
 
-            if (m_sWaylandConnection.dma.done)
+            if (m_sWaylandConnection.dma.done) {
+                close(fd);
                 return;
+            }
 
             m_vDMABUFMods.clear();
 
             m_sWaylandConnection.dma.formatTable = mmap(NULL, size, PROT_READ, MAP_PRIVATE, fd, 0);
 
+            close(fd);
+
             if (m_sWaylandConnection.dma.formatTable == MAP_FAILED) {
                 Debug::log(ERR, "[core] format table failed to mmap");
                 m_sWaylandConnection.dma.formatTable     = nullptr;
